; UR FINKEL - the songs, as text.
;
; Compile with `make music`, which writes src/song.h.  A tick is one PAL
; frame (1/50 s), so :50 is one second.
;
; TED has two tone voices, so every song here is exactly two lines of
; melody and harmony.  The harmony rule inherited from the BASIC edition
; is thirds and sixths below the melody within the scale - the intervals
; that give standard, pleasing colour - with fifths only at a cadence,
; and no seconds or sevenths anywhere.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; theme - the one the BASIC edition plays at victory, note for note.
; A-A-B-A' in D Dorian, with the B phrase borrowing the Hijaz tetrachord
; (a-bb-c#-d) for the Egyptian inflection, and a raised third under the
; final D for a warm Mediterranean close.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
song theme
  tempo 48
  grid  4
  meter 4
  bar
    1    d5    f4
    1&   e5    g4
    1a   f5    a4
    2e   e5    c5
    2&   d5    b4
    3    c5    a4
    3e   a4    f4
    3&   d5    f4
    4e   d5    f4
    4a   e5    g4
  bar
    1    f5    a4
    1&   e5    c5
    1a   d5    b4
    2e   c5    a4
    2&   a4    f4
    2a   d5    f4
    3&   a4    f4
    4    a#4   d4
    4e   c#5   a4
    4a   d5    f4
  bar
    1    c#5   a4
    1&   a#4   g4
    1a   a4    f4
    2    a4    d4
    2a   d5    f4
    3e   c5    e4
    3&   b4    g4
    4    a4    f4
    4e   g4    e4
    4a   f4    d4
  bar
    1    e4    c4
    1e   d5    f#4
    end  2e

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; fanfare - six harmony pairs, one per curtain step: a rising D major call
; resolving through the dominant to a held tonic. Quarter notes, with the
; finish held for a full second.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
song fanfare
  v1
    f#4:25 a4:25 d5:25 e5:25 e5:12 d5:60
  v2
    d4:25 f#4:25 f#4:25 c#5:25 c#5:12 f#4:60

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; cheer_a / cheer_b - six notes each, sounded one per step of the
; turn-transition colour sweep, so the ear knows whose turn begins without
; reading anything.  A rising major call for player one, a modal minor
; turn for player two.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
song cheer_a
  tempo 24
  grid  4
  meter 4
  bar
    1    d4    d3
    1e   f#4   d3
    1&   a4    d3
    1a   c#5   a3
    2    e5    a3
    2e   c#5   a3
    end  2&

song cheer_b
  tempo 24
  grid  4
  meter 4
  bar
    1    a4    a3
    1e   c5    a3
    1&   b4    e3
    1a   e5    e3
    2    d5    a3
    2e   c5    a3
    end  2&

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; the bed - the generated ambient layer that plays under a match.
;
; Two instruments, because TED has two voices: a PRIMARY that arpeggiates
; and a SECONDARY that sustains.  Each owns four banks, a to d.  The
; primary walks a-b-c-d one bank per phrase; the secondary changes every
; third phrase, and 4 against 3 is coprime, so the pairing takes twelve
; phrases - about eight minutes - to come round again.
;
; Every step is a chord DEGREE, not a pitch: 1 root, 3 third, 5 fifth,
; 8 octave, 9 ninth.  So a bank can only ever name notes that are in the
; bar's chord, and no pairing of banks can be dissonant.  Quality is not
; written here - the bed flips between major and minor thirds itself.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; the chord per bar, in D dorian: i bVII IV v, twice, landing home
progression  i bVII IV v i bVII bIII v

; primary: the arpeggio.  "up" adds semitones, "div" is ticks per step
; before the phrase arch bends it - smaller is faster.
bank primary a  1 3 5          up 0   div 7      ; the plain triad
bank primary b  1 5 3 8        up 0   div 5      ; wider, and quicker
bank primary c  1 3 5 8 5 3    up 0   div 3      ; an arch, in the same octave
bank primary d  1 8            up 0   div 11     ; spare: root and octave

; secondary: the sustained voice.  Two degrees, alternating bar by bar,
; which is how a hurdy-gurdy's two drone strings are tuned.
bank second a  1 1                                ; a root pedal
bank second b  1 5                                ; root and fifth by turns
bank second c  5 5                                ; a fifth pedal
bank second d  3 1                                ; third, then root

; how long each instrument holds a bank before the next one takes over.
; Keep these coprime: four banks at 1 against four banks at 3 do not pair
; up the same way twice for twelve phrases, and that long structure is the
; only thing standing between an eight-minute match and a loop you notice.
rate primary 1
rate second  3

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; hurrian - after the Hurrian Hymn No. 6, Ugarit, c. 1400 BCE: the oldest
; complete composition we have.  The cuneiform names intervals rather than
; pitches, so every modern rendering is an interpretation; this follows the
; Dumbrill reading, in the two-voice form the tablet's interval names imply.
;
; The paired notes of that reading fall out well here for a reason worth
; recording: the tablet notates INTERVALS - two notes at once - and TED has
; exactly two voices.  So v2 takes the lower note of each dyad and holds it
; until the next one rather than resting, which turns the pairs into a
; sustained lower line under the melody.
;
; The two voices carry the same number of notes at the same lengths, which
; is not decoration: the player loops each voice independently when it
; reaches its own end, so voices of unequal total length would drift apart
; a little more on every repeat.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
song intro
  ; THE WAY IN.  Five seconds of trumpets, and the only piece here written
  ; for two voices as HARMONY rather than as melody-over-bass: v1 and v2
  ; move together in thirds and fifths, which is what a pair of natural
  ; trumpets does and what makes six notes sound like a fanfare rather than
  ; a tune.
  ;
  ; Bars 1-2 are the call: a rising triad on the beat, the shape every
  ; royal entrance has had since somebody first had two trumpets.  Bar 3
  ; answers it a fourth higher.  Bar 4 is the CHIMES - the two voices an
  ; octave apart, struck on the beat and left to ring, which on two square
  ; waves with no envelope is as close to a bell as this machine gets.
  ;
  ; tempo 16 is 187 bpm, which is brisk for a fanfare and right for one
  ; that has five seconds to be over in: four bars land at 248 frames, just
  ; under five seconds.  The laser cuts UR underneath it and finishes
  ; first, deliberately - the letters are standing before the last chime.
  tempo 16
  grid  2
  meter 4
  bar
    1    d4    d3
    2    f#4   d3
    3    a4    d3
    4    d5    a3
  bar
    1    a4    f#3
    2    d5    f#3
    3    f#5   a3
    4    a5    d4
    4&   =     =
  bar
    1    g4    g3
    2    b4    g3
    3    d5    b3
    4    g5    d4
    4&   =     =
  bar
    1    d5    d4
    2    =     =
    3    a5    a4
    4    d6    d5
    end  4&

song select
  ; THE CHIME that answers a keypress on the menu.  Two notes, struck
  ; together a fifth apart and left to ring - the shortest thing in this
  ; file, because it has to land before the player has finished taking
  ; their finger off the key or it reads as a delay rather than an answer.
  ;
  ; tempo 12 is 250 bpm; six rows of a sixteenth is 18 frames, a third of
  ; a second, which is about as long as an acknowledgement can be before
  ; it becomes an event.
  tempo 12
  grid  4
  meter 4
  bar
    1    d5    g4
    1&   a5    d5
    2&   =     =
    end  3

song rising
  ; THE WAY IN to a mode: an ascending run under the closing curtain, both
  ; voices climbing in parallel sixths so it opens out rather than merely
  ; going up.  It ends unresolved, on the fifth, because what follows it is
  ; the game starting - a rise that lands would be a full stop.
  tempo 20
  grid  4
  meter 4
  bar
    1    d4    f#3
    1e   f#4   a3
    1&   a4    d4
    1a   d5    f#4
    2    f#5   a4
    2e   a5    d5
    2&   d6    f#5
    3    a5    d5
    3&   =     =
    end  4
